<!DOCTYPE html>
<html class="brand-color-background antialiased" lang="en" data-theme="light" style="font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />

    <!-- Primary Meta Tags -->
    <title>Free Readability Checker - Test Reading Level and Grade - Coffee &amp; Fun LLC</title>
    <meta name="title" content="Free Readability Checker - Test Reading Level and Grade - Coffee &amp; Fun LLC" />
    <meta name="description" content="Paste your writing and see its reading level in plain English, with Flesch Reading Ease, Flesch Kincaid grade, Gunning Fog, SMOG, Coleman Liau and ARI scores, plus the exact sentences and words to fix. Free, no sign up, runs in your browser." />
    <meta name="author" content="Coffee &amp; Fun LLC" />
    <meta name="robots" content="index, follow" />

    <!-- Canonical URL -->
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/" />

    <!-- Favicon -->
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/images/brand/favicon.png" sizes="any" />

    <!-- Open Graph -->
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Coffee &amp; Fun" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Free Readability Checker - Test Reading Level and Grade" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="See your writing's reading level in plain English, and the exact sentences and words worth fixing. Free and private, nothing leaves your browser." />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/assets/images/social/pages/readability-checker.png" />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content="Readability Checker card showing a reading level gauge and the Coffee and Fun cup logo" />
    <meta property="og:locale" content="en_US" />

    <!-- Twitter Card -->
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:site" content="@bycoffeeandfun" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="Free Readability Checker - Test Reading Level and Grade" />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="See your writing's reading level in plain English, and the exact sentences and words worth fixing. Free and private, nothing leaves your browser." />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/assets/images/social/pages/readability-checker.png" />
    <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="Readability Checker card showing a reading level gauge and the Coffee and Fun cup logo" />

    <meta name="theme-color" content="#fef5ec" />

    <!-- Structured Data -->
    <script type="application/ld+json">
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@graph": [
          {
            "@type": "WebApplication",
            "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/#app",
            "name": "Readability Checker",
            "alternateName": "Reading Level Test",
            "description": "Check the reading level of any text with six readability formulas, and see which sentences and words are making it harder to read.",
            "url": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/",
            "applicationCategory": "UtilitiesApplication",
            "operatingSystem": "Web Browser",
            "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
            "featureList": [
              "Flesch Reading Ease score",
              "Flesch Kincaid grade level",
              "Gunning Fog index",
              "SMOG index",
              "Coleman Liau index",
              "Automated Readability Index",
              "Plain English reading level verdict",
              "Longest sentences listed for rewriting",
              "Complex word list with syllable counts",
              "Word, sentence, syllable and reading time counts",
              "Runs entirely in the browser with no uploads"
            ],
            "isAccessibleForFree": true,
            "inLanguage": "en",
            "publisher": { "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/#organization" },
            "creator": { "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/#founder" },
            "isPartOf": { "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/#website" }
          },
          {
            "@type": "FAQPage",
            "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/#faq",
            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "My score went up but the writing feels worse. What happened?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Readability formulas count sentence length and syllables. They cannot tell whether a sentence still makes sense. Chopping everything in half will raise your score and can leave prose that reads like a telegram. Use the score to find candidates for rewriting, then judge the rewrite by reading it, not by watching the number move."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Is writing for a lower grade the same as dumbing it down?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No. A lower grade means less effort to decode, not less to say. Specialists read plain writing faster than dense writing on their own subject, and they are usually the ones reading in a hurry. You are removing friction, not content. If an idea is genuinely complex, keep the idea and simplify the sentence carrying it."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "My subject has long words I cannot avoid. Does that mean I fail?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Not really. These formulas count syllables, not familiarity, so photosynthesis costs the same as any other five syllable word even though most people met it at school. If a technical term is the right word, keep it. Shorten the sentence around it instead, and the score comes down while the meaning stays put."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What should I paste in? Do headings and bullet points count?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Paste the prose you actually want measured. Headings and bullet fragments often have no full stop, so the tool joins them onto whatever follows and reports one long sentence you never wrote. For the most accurate reading, leave out navigation, captions and code, and check your body text on its own."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What can these scores not see?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Quite a lot. They cannot tell whether your paragraphs are in a sensible order, whether a sentence is ambiguous, whether your reader already knows your jargon, or whether what you wrote is true. A confusing argument in short sentences scores well. Treat the number as one signal and read the words."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does reading level matter for accessibility?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "It can. WCAG 2.2 includes a success criterion called Reading Level, numbered 3.1.5, at the AAA level. Where text demands more reading ability than lower secondary education, which WCAG puts at seven to nine years of schooling, it asks you to offer a simpler version or a summary. Plain writing also helps people with dyslexia, people reading in a second language, and anyone who is tired or distracted."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does readability affect Google rankings?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Not directly. Google has never listed a readability score as a ranking factor, and plenty of dense pages rank well. What it does affect is what happens after someone arrives: how far they read, whether they understand you, and whether they do the thing you wanted. That is usually worth more than the position."
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
            "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/#breadcrumbs",
            "itemListElement": [
              { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/" },
              { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Apps and tools", "item": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/apps/" },
              { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Readability Checker", "item": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/" }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    </script>

    <script src="https://unpkg.com/vue@3"></script>

    {% bundledCss %}
    {% pageCss "readability-checker" %}
    {% include sketch-styles.liquid %}
    {% include schema-org.liquid %}
</head>

<body>
    {% include header.liquid %}
{% raw %}
    <a href="#tool" class="skip">Skip to the checker</a>

    <main v-cloak id="main-content" class="wrap">

        <header id="tool" class="page-head">
            <!-- Sized like every other page title. At the inherited 16px the mocha
                 brand heading measured 4.18:1, which fails AA for normal text. Past
                 24px it counts as large text, where 3:1 applies and it clears. -->
            <h1 class="brand-heading text-3xl sm:text-4xl">Readability checker</h1>
            <p class="lede">
                Paste your writing below and see how hard it is to read, in plain English rather than
                jargon. You get a reading level, six standard readability scores, and the exact
                sentences and words worth changing. Nothing you type leaves your browser.
            </p>
        </header>

        <!-- Live status for screen readers -->
        <div class="sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite">{{ announcement }}</div>

        <!-- Input on the left, answer on the right, so pasting produces a visible
             change at the same vertical position instead of below the fold. The
             empty state sits where the answer will appear, which is the cue that
             tells a first time visitor where to look. -->
        <div class="cf-work grid items-start gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">

            <!-- ===== the text ===== -->
            <section class="card cf-panel cf-input-card lg:sticky lg:top-36" aria-labelledby="h-text">
                <div class="card-body cf-input-body">
                    <h2 class="step-label" id="h-text">Your text</h2>

                    <label class="sr-only" for="rc-input">The text you want to check</label>
                    <textarea id="rc-input" ref="input" class="textarea w-full cf-input" v-model="text"
                              placeholder="Paste or type your writing here…"
                              aria-describedby="rc-stats" spellcheck="true"></textarea>

                    <div class="io-row">
                        <span class="counter" id="rc-stats">{{ statsLine }}</span>
                        <span class="io-actions">
                            <button v-if="text" type="button" class="link cf-linkish" @click="clearText">Clear</button>
                        </span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="samples">
                        <span class="samples-label" id="h-samples">Or try an example:</span>
                        <span class="join" role="group" aria-labelledby="h-samples">
                            <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline join-item cf-sample" style="--btn-color: var(--color-espresso); --btn-fg: #fff" @click="loadSample('simple')">Simple</button>
                            <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline join-item cf-sample" style="--btn-color: var(--color-espresso); --btn-fg: #fff" @click="loadSample('average')">Average</button>
                            <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline join-item cf-sample" style="--btn-color: var(--color-espresso); --btn-fg: #fff" @click="loadSample('complex')">Complex</button>
                        </span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ===== the answer ===== -->
            <div class="cf-answer">

                <section aria-labelledby="h-result">
                    <h2 class="step-label" id="h-result">How it reads</h2>

                    <div v-if="!hasEnough" class="alert cf-empty"
                         style="--alert-color: #fffcf7; --alert-border-color: var(--color-mocha)">
                        <p>Add at least ten words and your reading level will appear here as you type.</p>
                    </div>

                    <template v-else>
                        <div class="card card-lg cf-panel cf-verdict" :class="'band-' + band.label.toLowerCase().replace(/ /g, '-')">
                            <div class="card-body cf-verdict-body">
                                <p class="verdict-line">
                                    Reads like <strong>{{ verdict.grade }}</strong>
                                </p>
                                <p class="verdict-band">{{ verdict.label }}</p>
                                <p class="verdict-who">{{ verdict.who }}.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="stats stats-vertical sm:stats-horizontal w-full cf-panel cf-quickstats">
                            <div class="stat cf-stat">
                                <div class="stat-title cf-stat-label">words</div>
                                <div class="stat-value cf-stat-value">{{ wordCount }}</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="stat cf-stat">
                                <div class="stat-title cf-stat-label">sentences</div>
                                <div class="stat-value cf-stat-value">{{ sentenceCount }}</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="stat cf-stat">
                                <div class="stat-title cf-stat-label">to read</div>
                                <div class="stat-value cf-stat-value">{{ readingTime }}</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="stat cf-stat">
                                <div class="stat-title cf-stat-label">long words</div>
                                <div class="stat-value cf-stat-value">{{ complexPercent }}%</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="result-actions">
                            <button type="button" class="btn btn-lg" style="--btn-color: var(--color-cocoa); --btn-fg: #fff" @click="copySummary">
                                {{ copied ? 'Copied ✓' : 'Copy the summary' }}
                            </button>
                        </div>
                    </template>
                </section>

                <!-- ===== what to change ===== -->
                <section v-if="hasEnough" aria-labelledby="h-fix">
                    <h2 class="step-label" id="h-fix">What to change</h2>

                    <div class="cf-notes">
                        <!-- The inner div is load bearing: .alert is grid-auto-flow:column
                             and lays two element children out side by side. -->
                        <div v-for="s in suggestions" :key="s.id" class="alert cf-note"
                             style="--alert-color: #fffcf7; --alert-border-color: var(--color-caramel)">
                            <div>
                                <h3 class="cf-note-title">{{ s.title }}</h3>
                                <p class="cf-note-detail">{{ s.detail }}</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div v-if="longSentences.length" class="fixlist">
                        <h3 class="fixlist-title">
                            Your longest sentences
                            <span class="fixlist-count">{{ longSentences.length }} over 25 words</span>
                        </h3>
                        <ol class="sentence-list">
                            <li v-for="(s, i) in longSentences" :key="i">
                                <span class="badge badge-sm rounded-full cf-len"
                                      style="--badge-color: var(--color-mocha); --badge-bg: #f3e6d8; --badge-fg: var(--color-espresso)">{{ s.words }} words</span>
                                <span class="sentence-text">{{ s.text }}</span>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>

                    <div v-if="complexWords.length" class="fixlist">
                        <h3 class="fixlist-title">
                            Longest words
                            <span class="fixlist-count">{{ complexWords.length }} with 3+ syllables</span>
                        </h3>
                        <ul class="cf-chips">
                            <li v-for="w in complexWords.slice(0, 24)" :key="w.word"
                                class="badge badge-lg rounded-full"
                                style="--badge-color: var(--color-mocha); --badge-bg: #f3e6d8; --badge-fg: var(--color-espresso)">
                                <span class="wl-word">{{ w.word }}</span>
                                <span class="wl-syll">{{ w.syllables }} syllables</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- ===== the formulas, deliberately last and folded away ===== -->
                <section v-if="hasEnough" aria-labelledby="h-scores">
                    <details class="collapse collapse-arrow cf-panel cf-scores">
                        <summary class="collapse-title cf-scores-title">
                            <h2 class="step-label cf-scores-head" id="h-scores">The six scores</h2>
                        </summary>
                        <div class="collapse-content">
                            <p class="section-note">
                                Each formula measures something slightly different. The reading level above is the
                                average of the ones that apply to your text.
                            </p>

                            <div class="cf-formula-grid">
                                <div v-for="f in formulaRows" :key="f.key" class="card cf-panel cf-panel-soft">
                                    <div class="card-body cf-formula-body">
                                        <p class="formula-name">{{ f.name }}</p>
                                        <p v-if="!f.unavailable" class="formula-value">
                                            {{ f.value }}<span class="formula-unit" v-if="f.unit === 'grade'"> grade</span>
                                        </p>
                                        <p v-else class="formula-value formula-na">Not yet</p>
                                        <p class="formula-plain">{{ f.why || f.plain }}</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </section>

            </div>
        </div>
{% endraw %}

        <!-- ===== Reference and FAQ: real content, and what this page ranks for ===== -->
        <section class="cf-ref" aria-labelledby="h-guide">
            <h2 class="step-label" id="h-guide">What the grade levels mean</h2>
            <p class="section-note">
                Readability formulas report a US school grade. It is a rough guide to how much effort
                a reader has to spend, not a judgement of how good the writing is.
            </p>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto cf-panel">
                <table class="table cf-band-table">
                    <caption class="sr-only">Reading grade levels and the audiences they suit</caption>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">Grade</th>
                            <th scope="col">Difficulty</th>
                            <th scope="col">Who reads it comfortably</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><th scope="row">1 to 5</th><td>Very easy</td><td>Almost everyone, including younger readers</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">6 to 8</th><td>Easy</td><td>Most adults read this without effort</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">9 to 10</th><td>Fairly easy</td><td>Comfortable for a general audience</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">11 to 12</th><td>Moderate</td><td>Fine for most adults who are paying attention</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">13 to 14</th><td>Hard</td><td>Assumes a confident, motivated reader</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">15 to 17</th><td>Very hard</td><td>Degree level. Many readers will give up</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">18 and up</th><td>Extremely hard</td><td>Specialist or academic readers only</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cf-ref" aria-labelledby="h-faq">
            <h2 class="step-label" id="h-faq">Common questions</h2>

            <div class="cf-faq">
                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow cf-panel cf-faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">My score went up but the writing feels worse. What happened?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>Readability formulas count sentence length and syllables. They cannot tell whether
                        a sentence still makes sense. Chopping everything in half will raise your score and
                        can leave prose that reads like a telegram. Use the score to find candidates for
                        rewriting, then judge the rewrite by reading it, not by watching the number move.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow cf-panel cf-faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">Is writing for a lower grade the same as dumbing it down?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>No. A lower grade means less effort to decode, not less to say. Specialists read
                        plain writing faster than dense writing on their own subject, and they are usually the
                        ones reading in a hurry. You are removing friction, not content. If an idea is
                        genuinely complex, keep the idea and simplify the sentence carrying it.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow cf-panel cf-faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">My subject has long words I cannot avoid. Does that mean I fail?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>Not really. These formulas count syllables, not familiarity, so photosynthesis
                        costs the same as any other five syllable word even though most people met it at
                        school. If a technical term is the right word, keep it. Shorten the sentence around it
                        instead, and the score comes down while the meaning stays put.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow cf-panel cf-faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">What should I paste in? Do headings and bullet points count?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>Paste the prose you actually want measured. Headings and bullet fragments often
                        have no full stop, so the tool joins them onto whatever follows and reports one long
                        sentence you never wrote. For the most accurate reading, leave out navigation,
                        captions and code, and check your body text on its own.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow cf-panel cf-faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">What can these scores not see?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>Quite a lot. They cannot tell whether your paragraphs are in a sensible order,
                        whether a sentence is ambiguous, whether your reader already knows your jargon, or
                        whether what you wrote is true. A confusing argument in short sentences scores well.
                        Treat the number as one signal and read the words.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow cf-panel cf-faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">Does reading level matter for accessibility?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>It can. WCAG 2.2 includes a success criterion called Reading Level, numbered 3.1.5,
                        at the AAA level. Where text demands more reading ability than lower secondary
                        education, which WCAG puts at seven to nine years of schooling, it asks you to offer a
                        simpler version or a summary. Plain
                        writing also helps people with dyslexia, people reading in a second language, and
                        anyone who is tired or distracted.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow cf-panel cf-faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">Does readability affect Google rankings?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>Not directly. Google has never listed a readability score as a ranking factor, and
                        plenty of dense pages rank well. What it does affect is what happens after someone
                        arrives: how far they read, whether they understand you, and whether they do the thing
                        you wanted. That is usually worth more than the position.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>
        </section>

        <p class="foot">Everything is worked out on your device. Nothing you paste here is uploaded, stored or logged.</p>
    </main>

    {% include modals.liquid %}
    {% include footer.liquid %}

    {% pageJs "readability-checker" %}
</body>
</html>
